"About 2,400 to 2,500 years ago, in the plains of the Ganges River in northern India, a teacher named Siddhartha Gautama began to gather followers. They called him the Buddha, “the awakened one.” He taught that suffering has causes and that a path of ethical living, meditation, and wisdom can bring it to an end. Scholars do not agree on when he lived: an older view puts his life at about 560 to 480 BCE, while many now think he died around 400 BCE.": "5b2280c88465",
"About 2,400 to 2,500 years ago, in the plains of the Ganges River in northern India, a teacher named Siddhartha Gautama began to gather followers. They called him the Buddha, “the awakened one.” He taught that suffering has causes and that a path of ethical living, meditation, and wisdom can bring it to an end. Scholars do not agree on when he lived: an older view puts his life at about 560 to 480 BCE, while many now think he died around 400 BCE. For centuries, the Buddha's teachings were not written down. Monks and nuns learned them by heart and recited them together. Sri Lankan tradition says the Pāli scriptures were first written down on the island in the 1st century BCE. The oldest Buddhist manuscripts we actually have, written on birch bark, come from Gandhara in today's Pakistan and date from about the 1st century BCE to the 3rd century CE. The earliest datable written evidence for Buddhism comes from Ashoka, who ruled most of South Asia from about 270 to 232 BCE (some sources give 265 to 238). In one of his own edicts, he says he conquered the land of Kalinga, that 100,000 people were killed, and that he felt deep remorse. He had edicts carved on rocks and pillars across his empire, most in the Brahmi script and a few in other scripts and languages, including Greek and Aramaic. One edict speaks of his faith in the Buddha; another asks people to respect the followers of all religions. From India, Buddhism traveled south and east by sea. Sri Lankan chronicles say Ashoka's son Mahinda brought it to the island in the 3rd century BCE. Later, ships carried monks and traders across the Bay of Bengal. In 671 CE the Chinese monk Yijing studied in Srivijaya, on Sumatra, before sailing on to India, and in the 8th and 9th centuries the great stone monument of Borobudur rose on Java. Buddhism also traveled north and east over land. In Gandhara, artists carved some of the first images of the Buddha in human form in the 1st century CE. By 65 CE a Han prince in China was making offerings to the Buddha, and from about 148 CE, according to a later catalog of translations, monks such as An Shigao were translating texts into Chinese. Chinese pilgrims walked to India to find more: Xuanzang left in 629 and came home in 645 with 657 texts. The teaching reached Korea in the 4th century, Japan in the 6th, and Tibet in the 7th and 8th, according to each land's histories. Each place made Buddhism its own, in its own language, art, and buildings. In India itself, Buddhism slowly declined, and the great monastery of Nalanda was sacked around 1200. Today about 324 million people, roughly 4 percent of the world, are counted as Buddhists, and almost all of them live in the Asia-Pacific region. The teaching traveled with monks, traders, pilgrims, and rulers who chose to support it, over about 1,500 years.": "675f6cdbe923",
"According to the chronicles, Mahinda went to Sri Lanka.": "b10a64305f3b",
"Buddhism also traveled north and east over land. In Gandhara, artists carved some of the first images of the Buddha in human form in the 1st century CE. By 65 CE a Han prince in China was making offerings to the Buddha, and from about 148 CE, according to a later catalog of translations, monks such as An Shigao were translating texts into Chinese. Chinese pilgrims walked to India to find more: Xuanzang left in 629 and came home in 645 with 657 texts. The teaching reached Korea in the 4th century, Japan in the 6th, and Tibet in the 7th and 8th, according to each land's histories.": "b3bcd0eb7462",
